# 4. Documentation: state the actual enforced deadline model and its deliberate
# host-bound containment semantics without claiming arbitrary subprocess support.
replace_once(
    'docs/AURA_VERIFIED_EPHEMERAL_WORKSPACE_PR2.md',
    '''Nodes are executed in sorted topological waves. PR2 records how many nodes are\nproven independent but intentionally uses deterministic serial execution\n(`parallelism_used: 1`) until a later bounded implementation proves safe\nconcurrency behavior.\n''',
    '''Nodes are executed in sorted topological waves. PR2 records how many nodes are\nproven independent but intentionally uses deterministic serial execution\n(`parallelism_used: 1`) until a later bounded implementation proves safe\nconcurrency behavior.\n\nExecutable V2 callbacks are additionally bounded by one runtime-owned absolute\ndeadline: the minimum of the node timeout, the cumulative workspace wall-time\nbudget (measured from activation), and the workspace TTL. On POSIX hosts with\n`fork`, the existing adapter registry runs only the exact already-registered\ncallable in an isolated child process group and the parent continuously checks\nthe live workspace lease/state and current adapter identity. Deadline expiry,\nlease revocation, cancellation, or binding drift kills and reaps the whole child\nprocess group before the result can be accepted. Hosts without that containment\nprimitive fail V2 bounded execution closed. This is internal containment of an\nadmitted adapter, not shell/subprocess capability and not arbitrary native\nexecution. Callers that do not opt into the V2 deadline contract retain the V1\nregistry's historical direct-call behavior.\n''',
)

replace_once(
    'docs/AURA_VERIFIED_EPHEMERAL_WORKSPACE_PR2.md',
    '''Failures are classified as `local`, `upstream`, `structural`, `stale`, `policy`,\n`budget`, `cancellation`, or `environment`. Ordinary adapter exceptions are\nnormalized into a failed result. `KeyboardInterrupt`, `SystemExit`, and other\nprocess-level exceptions are never swallowed: cleanup runs and the original\nexception is re-raised, with a cleanup exception explicitly chained if cleanup\nitself fails.\n''',
    '''Failures are classified as `local`, `upstream`, `structural`, `stale`, `policy`,\n`budget`, `cancellation`, or `environment`. Ordinary adapter exceptions are\nnormalized into a failed result. Node/workspace wall-time expiry is a `budget`\nfailure; absolute workspace TTL expiry is `stale`. `KeyboardInterrupt`,\n`SystemExit`, and other process-level exceptions are never swallowed: bounded\nexecution reports the interruption to the parent runtime, cleanup runs, and the\nprocess-level exception is re-raised.\n''',
)

